{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>A just balance and scales of judgment are HaShem's; all the weights of the bag are His work (Proverbs 16:11).</b>" "<b>Solomon</b> the king, peace be upon him, intended in this verse (Proverbs 16) that all of a person's deeds are weighed, and all the weights belong to the Name, exalted be He. The great, single weight is the פלס (the level/plumb line), and also the small weight, which consists of two, and therefore they are called מאזנים (scales) in the dual form, like the terms found in the Writings in this matter that indicate pairs. And behold, He, exalted be He, weighs all the particulars of deeds with כל אבני כיס — all the stones of the bag. The intention of this is that just as He possesses all the weights from the greatest to the smallest, He possesses all the stones from the greatest to the smallest among them — as when you speak of the ככר (talent), which is the measure of the great weight, down to the אונקיא (uncia) and half an uncia and a third of it and a quarter of it, and so on to the end of the smallest measure in the extreme. And all of this is a parable to understand and to instruct that He, exalted be He, weighs all transgressions — the grave and the light — and punishes them according to the magnitude of the grave transgression and according to the smallness of the light transgression, and nothing is hidden from before Him, exalted be He. And a proof that He, blessed be He, returns to weigh the light transgressions after having weighed the grave ones is what the prophet said in the name of the Holy One, blessed be He (Jeremiah 16:16): 'הנני שולח לרבים דיגים נאם ה' ודיגום ואחרי כן אשלח לרבים צידים וצדום' — He mentioned the language of hunting after the language of fishing, like a person who clears out the vessels of the house and goes back and checks again over them whether anything of them still remains there. And the meaning of the verse is that the פלס and the מאזנים and כל אבני כיס which belong to HaShem — all of it is משפט, that is to say, true judgment. And the reason He mentioned the expression כיס (bag) is that the practice is to place the stones inside the bag so that they remain in their cleanliness and do not receive any dirt, and so that they not be worn down by the constant use that a person makes of them, and so that they remain precise in their weight." "<b>And it is possible</b> to interpret further that the expression כיס (bag) includes an additional meaning, for stones are hidden and concealed inside the bag and no person knows what is within it, for they are stored away and hidden there; so too the judgment of HaShem in His weighing-stones is hidden and concealed from mortals — it is impossible for a person to know this, for the power of human intellect is meager and cannot grasp how He, exalted be He, weighs the deeds of the righteous and the wicked. And this is what David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 36:7): 'צדקתך כהררי אל משפטיך תהום רבה' — the verse explained that the righteousness of HaShem and His kindnesses and His mercies are lofty and renowned like the mountains of El, renowned and revealed to the eye of all, but His judgments are hidden and concealed like תהום רבה (the great deep), of which no person knows its end. And this is what is said in the Midrash: 'צדקתך כהררי אל משפטיך תהום רבה' — when You bring righteousness into the world it is publicized like these mountains, but the judgments are hidden like תהום רבה.' How so? When Jerusalem was destroyed it was destroyed on the ninth of Av, and when He showed the calamity to Ezekiel He showed it to him on the tenth, on the fifth of the month; but when He came to make Israel great He publicized Israel — in what place? In the wilderness of Sinai. On what day? On the first of the month. In what month? In the second month. In what year? In the second year. For what matter? 'לצאתם מארץ מצרים' — to their going out from the land of Egypt. And this is what is written:" "<b>And HaShem spoke to Moses in the wilderness of Sinai, in the tent of meeting, on the first of the second month, in the second year after their going out from the land of Egypt, saying.</b> In the wilderness of Sinai — where Mount Sinai was." "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> 'In the wilderness of Sinai' — the Torah was given in three things: in fire, in water, and in wilderness. In fire, as it is written (Exodus 19:17–18): 'והר סיני עשן כלו מפני אשר ירד עליו ה' באש.' In water, as it is written (Judges 5:4): 'ה' בצאתך משעיר בצעדך משדה אדום ארץ רעשה גם שמים נטפו גם עבים נטפו מים.' In wilderness, as it is written: 'וידבר ה' אל משה במדבר סיני.' And why was it given in these three things? To tell you: just as these are free to all who come into the world, so too the Torah is free to all who come into the world. And as it is written (Isaiah 55:1): 'הוי כל צמא לכו למים.' And why in the wilderness? To tell you that a person does not acquire the Torah until he makes himself כמדבר — ownerless like the wilderness." "<b>In the Tent of Meeting.</b> This is the Tabernacle, and from the time He called to him from there, as it is said (Leviticus 1:1): ״ויקרא אל משה וידבר ה׳ אליו מאהל מועד״, He did not speak with him except from there, because the Glory that had been on Mount Sinai was transferred there. Therefore He commands here to set bounds around the Tabernacle, that the Levites should camp around it, and the stranger who approaches shall die, just as He set bounds around the mountain and said (Exodus 19:13): ״לא תגע בו יד״, and He warned concerning the Tabernacle (Numbers 18:5): ״ושמרתם את משמרת הקדש ואת משמרת המזבח״, just as He warned at Mount Sinai (Exodus 19:24): ״והכהנים והעם אל יהרסו״, and He commanded concerning it that they should not come to see, just as He commanded at Mount Sinai (Exodus 19): ״והכהנים והעם אל יהרסו לעלות אל ה׳״. And further He commands that the Levites camp around the Tent of Meeting, and they were twenty-two thousand Levites, just as twenty-two thousand ministering angels descended with Him on Mount Sinai, as it is written (Psalms 68:18): ״רכב אלהים רבותים״ etc., and Israel were four banners, just as they saw four camps of the Shekhinah on Mount Sinai." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> In the Tent of Meeting — until the Tent of Meeting was established, He spoke with him in the bush, as it is said (Exodus 3:4): ״ויקרא אליו אלהים מתוך הסנה״; afterward He spoke with him in Egypt, as it is said (Exodus 12): ״ויאמר אל משה ואל אהרן בארץ מצרים״; and afterward He spoke with him in Midian, as it is said (Exodus 4): ״ויאמר ה׳ אל משה במדין״; and afterward He spoke with him at Sinai, as it is said (Leviticus 25:1): ״וידבר ה׳ אל משה בהר סיני לאמר״. Once the Tent of Meeting was established, He said: modesty is fitting, as it is said (Micah 6:8): ״והצנע לכת עם אלהיך״ — He began speaking with him in the Tent of Meeting. And so David said (Psalms 45:14): ״כל כבודה בת מלך פנימה ממשבצות זהב לבושה״ — בת מלך, this is Moses, as it is said (Isaiah 19:4): ״וסכרתי את מצרים ביד אדנים קשה״ — these are the plagues that came upon the Egyptians; ומלך עז ימשל בם — this is Moses, who was the king of the Torah, which is called עז, as it is said (Psalms 29:11): ״ה׳ עוז לעמו יתן״, and it is written (Proverbs 8:15): ״בי מלכים ימלוכו״; therefore כל כבודה בת מלך פנימה. From here the Rabbis of blessed memory said: any woman who conducts herself with modesty, even an Israelite woman, is worthy of marrying a High Priest and raising up from him High Priests, as it is said (Psalms 45:14): ״ממשבצות זהב לבושה״. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: this is her glory, that He should speak in her presence, as it is said (Numbers 7:89): ״ובבא משה אל אהל מועד לדבר אתו וישמע את הקול״. Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi said: if the nations of the world knew how much the Holy Temple benefits them, they would surround it with legions to guard it, for it was more beneficial to them than it was to Israel. For Solomon too arranged in his prayer (2 Chronicles 6:32): ״וגם אל הנכרי אשר לא מעמך ישראל הוא״ etc., ״ואתה תשמע מן השמים ועשית ככל אשר יקרא אליך הנכרי״, whereas concerning Israel it is written (ibid.): ״ונתת לאיש ככל דרכיו אשר תדע את לבבו״. Solomon, peace be upon him, said: Master of the Worlds, if he is worthy, give to him, and if he is not worthy, do not give to him — because an Israelite knows the Holy One, blessed be He, and knows that the power is in His hand, and if his prayer is not heard he attributes it to himself and to his sin; but the foreigner brings a charge and says: A house whose name goes from one end of the world to the other — I wearied myself on many roads and came and prayed within it, and I found no substance in it, just as there is no substance in idolatry; therefore ״ועשית ככל אשר יקרא אליך הנכרי״. And you should not say that the Holy Temple was beneficial only to them, but even to Israel — for were it not for Israel, neither rain would descend upon the world nor the sun shine, except for their sake, as it is said (Jeremiah 33): ״אם לא בריתי״ etc. And in the time to come, the nations will see that the Holy One, blessed be He, speaks with Israel, and they too will come to cleave to them, as it is said (Zechariah 8:23): ״נלכה עמכם כי שמענו אלהים עמכם״." "<b>On the first day of the second month.</b> He commanded him concerning the arrangement of the banners — how they should journey and how they should camp — for they did not journey until the twentieth day of the second month. And as for why this passage of the census (חומש הפקודים) was juxtaposed to the passage of the substitute (תמורה) above, they said in the Midrash: to teach you — just as the Holy One, blessed be He, is One and has no substitute, as it is said (1 Samuel 2:2): ״אין קדוש כה׳ כי אין בלתך״, so Israel will not be exchanged for another nation. And this is what is written (Song of Songs 2:16): ״דודי לי ואני לו״ — I am holy and you are holy; do not exchange Me, just as I do not exchange you. You are called צאן (flock); just as one does not exchange one beast for another beast, so I do not exchange you."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {6 "Hebrew text reads 'באהל מועד' as the opening lemma, but the method-header is 'ובמדרש' (By way of the Midrash); the lemma is translated as its running text within the comment."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:30:12.614704497Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}